# GitHub Actions — Workflow Triggers

## What triggers are

A trigger tells GitHub Actions when to run your workflow. Every workflow must have at least one trigger defined under `on:`.

---

## Common triggers

### push — run on every commit pushed

```yaml
on: push
```

Fires whenever anyone pushes a commit to any branch. Most basic trigger.

### pull_request — run on PR activity

```yaml
on:
  pull_request:
    types: [opened, edited, closed, assigned]
```

Fires when a PR is opened, edited, closed, or assigned. If you omit `types:`, it defaults to `opened`, `synchronize`, and `reopened`.

### schedule — run on a cron schedule

```yaml
on:
  schedule:
    - cron: '30 5 * * 1-5'    # 5:30am every weekday
    - cron: '0 0 * * 0'       # midnight every Sunday
```

Cron syntax: `minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week`

Use [crontab.guru](https://crontab.guru) to build and test cron expressions visually.

### workflow_dispatch — manual trigger with a button

```yaml
on:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      environment:
        description: 'Target environment'
        required: true
      logLevel:
        description: 'Log level'
        required: true
        default: 'warning'
        type: choice
        options:
          - info
          - warning
          - debug
```

Adds a "Run workflow" button in the GitHub Actions UI. Inputs let you pass parameters at runtime. Reference them in steps:

```yaml
steps:
  - name: Show inputs
    run: |
      echo "Environment: ${{ inputs.environment }}"
      echo "Log level: ${{ inputs.logLevel }}"
```

### fork, issues, release

```yaml
on: fork      # fires when someone forks the repo
on: issues    # fires when issues are opened or modified
on: release   # fires on draft, published, or edited releases
```

